<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russian Orthodox Church</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> is the branch of Eastern Orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> that developed after the conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a> in the late tenth century. It became closely tied to Muscovite and then Russian imperial authority, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> placed it under state control by replacing the patriarchate with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-synod">Holy Synod</a> in 1721. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> treat Orthodoxy as a central pillar of tsarist legitimacy and identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church.jpg" alt="Russian Orthodox Church" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Crawshaw · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Basils_Cathedral-500px.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
